NAME
    vault - passphrase-based encryption for files, tapes, or data in place

SYNOPSIS
    vault encrypt <src> <dst> [passphrase | -]
    vault decrypt <src> <dst> [passphrase | -]
    vault encrypt-in-place <file> [passphrase | -]
    vault decrypt-in-place <file> [passphrase | -]
    vault info <file>
    vault tape encrypt|decrypt [passphrase | -]

DESCRIPTION
    vault encrypts and decrypts data with a passphrase. It protects data at
    rest — a stolen disk or tape reveals only ciphertext.

    Leave the passphrase off (or give -) and vault asks for it without
    showing it; encrypting asks twice, because a passphrase you mistyped
    unseen would lock the data for good. A passphrase typed on the command
    line works too, but it stays in the seat's command history ('history',
    the up arrow) for whoever sits down next.

    Encryption uses the data card's AES when available, with a key derived from
    your passphrase; integrity is verified on decrypt, so a wrong passphrase or a
    tampered file fails cleanly rather than producing garbage.

    For storing the passphrases themselves, see 'keychain' — a per-user stash
    unlocked by your login password, so you don't retype long passphrases.

EXAMPLES
    vault encrypt /home/me/secret.txt /home/me/secret.vlt
    vault decrypt /home/me/secret.vlt /home/me/secret.txt
    vault encrypt-in-place /home/me/notes.txt

